# apps/server configuration. Copy to apps/server/.env.local (gitignored) and fill in.
#
# This file is read by Node itself, through --env-file-if-exists=.env.local in
# the `dev`, `start`, `migrate` and `token:issue` scripts — NOT by Vite, which
# never touches apps/server. Two consequences:
#
#   - The path is exactly `apps/server/.env.local`, relative to this package.
#     A `.env.local` at the repo root is not read by the server.
#   - A variable already present in the real environment wins over this file,
#     so `DATABASE_URL=… pnpm -F server dev` still overrides.
#
# The test suite deliberately does NOT load this file: the whole point of a
# suite is that it says the same thing on every machine. See AGENTS.md.
#
# The server logs one line at boot saying whether hosted sign-in is on or off.
# If you set CLERK_JWT_KEY here and that line still says OFF, the file is not
# where the server looked or the variable is misspelled.

# Where Postgres lives. Defaults to the compose.yaml database on 127.0.0.1:5433
# when unset, which is what `pnpm db:up` starts.
# DATABASE_URL=postgres://taverns:taverns@127.0.0.1:5433/taverns

# The port the API binds. Defaults to 3000.
# PORT=3000

# Comma-separated origins allowed to call the API from a browser. Defaults to
# the Vite dev server's two spellings. This same list feeds the `azp` audience
# check on hosted session tokens, so the two cannot drift apart.
# ALLOWED_ORIGINS=http://localhost:5173,http://127.0.0.1:5173

# How often a quiet live-session stream emits a keep-alive, in seconds.
# Defaults to 20.
#
# The number that matters belongs to whatever sits in front of the server, not
# to the server: an idle connection is commonly cut at 60 seconds by nginx and
# at 30 or less by some CDNs, with nothing said to either end. Set this below
# whatever your proxy does. A client calls a stream dead after two missed beats.
# LIVE_HEARTBEAT_SECONDS=20

# Clerk's JWT public verification key, in PEM form:
# Dashboard -> API keys -> Show JWT public key -> PEM Public Key.
#
# OPTIONAL. Leave it unset and the server runs with no hosted sign-in at all —
# `pnpm -F server dev` works, the whole suite passes, and a session-token-shaped
# credential is simply unknown. Setting it is what turns sign-in on.
#
# NOT a secret: verification is the only thing this key can do. CLERK_SECRET_KEY
# is deliberately absent from this server and must not be added — tokens are
# verified offline, so leaking this entire file still mints nobody a session.
#
# Node's env-file parser keeps newlines inside a double-quoted value, so paste
# the PEM as-is across multiple lines between the quotes:
# CLERK_JWT_KEY="-----BEGIN PUBLIC KEY-----
# ...
# -----END PUBLIC KEY-----"

# The Clerk SDK phones home on development instances unless this is set.
# CLERK_TELEMETRY_DISABLED=1

# --- Hob, the assistant -------------------------------------------------------
#
# OPTIONAL, exactly as CLERK_JWT_KEY is. Leave these unset and the server runs
# with no assistant: it boots, the whole suite passes, and the Hob panel reports
# itself unavailable rather than offering a composer that drops questions. The
# server logs one line at boot saying whether Hob is ON or OFF, naming the model
# and the endpoint — never the key.
#
# Both must be set. An endpoint with no model name would fail on the first
# question from inside a stream, which is a far worse way to learn about a
# missing variable.

# An OpenAI-compatible base URL. The captain's decision is locally hosted
# models, so this is ordinarily loopback:
#   llama.cpp   llama-server --jinja -m model.gguf --port 8080  ->  :8080/v1
#   Ollama      ollama serve                                    ->  :11434/v1
#   LM Studio   lms server start                                ->  :1234/v1
# HOB_API_URL=http://127.0.0.1:8080/v1

# Which model to ask, as that endpoint names it. It must support tool calling —
# Hob answers only from tool results, so a model that cannot call one has
# nothing to say. Qwen2.5-Instruct and Qwen3 both work; llama.cpp needs --jinja.
# HOB_MODEL=qwen2.5-3b-instruct

# A key for the model endpoint, if it wants one. Most local servers ignore it;
# some (vLLM) insist on a placeholder. THE ONLY SECRET ON THIS PAGE — it is
# Redacted in the config and never reaches a log, and no key of any kind may be
# committed.
# HOB_API_KEY=

# The output cap sent with every generation request. Defaults to 4096.
#
# It is sent explicitly, always, and that habit is the point rather than the
# number: a provider package that does not recognise a model id caps output
# silently (see AGENTS.md on the beta-102 capability trap), and the first
# symptom is an answer cut off mid-sentence.
#
# IT COVERS REASONING TOKENS TOO, which is why the default is not smaller. A
# thinking model — Qwen3, and most capable local models now — spends this budget
# on deliberation nobody sees before it emits its first tool call, and one that
# runs out has called no tool and said nothing. Hob reports that in words now,
# but the fix is this number. Raise it further if answers keep ending there.
# HOB_MAX_TOKENS=4096
